# Manufacturing & Supply Chain Analysis

/*
Queries 1 through 8 look at the relational database of Production Orders and Quality Control Tests. First I queried how many batches were failing 
quality control (QC) tests based on the status of the production orders. The results showed that scheduled orders were failing QC tests which did not make sense
if the product batch did not exist yet. I found that there were many tests dated before the scheduled date of the work order. It must be an error
from how the data was generated, which I accounted for in subsequent queries. Unfortunately there are several completed batches that have failed QC tests, 
as well as batches in progress and on hold. I ran a report of how many units of product from compeleted production orders have failed QC tests and which type
of test. I then wanted to see if any product batches had failed multiple tests, but each failed batch had only failed one type of QC test. I then queried the 
proportion of test types from the failed tests. The purity QC test is the most failed test. Lastly, I looked at the rate of each product to fail a QC test. 
The highest rate was 8%. I also looked at which test the top two products failed at, which was taste and purity.  

Queries 9 and 10 run reports from the Raw Materials Inventory on if any materials need to be reordered and which supplier is the least expensive on average. 
As of July 17, 2026, none of the supplies need to be reordered. The supplier that is majority cheapest is S01. 

Queries 11 through 17 investigate questions about product shipments. ChocoExpress is the cheapest carrier the company uses; however, they are also responsible 
for the most amount of delayed shipments, especially on the routes to Berlin, the destination with a high number of delays in comparison to other locations.
The company should consider not using ChocoExpress when shipping to Berlin, so that they may lower the overall average amount of delayed packages.
Additionally, I produced a report on how long shipments take for each route, from 4.4 to 11.3 days on average. 

Please see below for the queries and more details 
*/


/* Query 1
How many batches failed their quality control test, by status of production?
There are 32 scheduled batches that failed, which doesn't make sense if they haven't started production yet. 
May need to account for tests that were performed before scheduled date as errors 
*/
SELECT PO.Status, COUNT(DISTINCT PO.BatchNumber) AS NumberOfBatches
FROM ProductOrders PO
JOIN QCTests QC
ON PO.BatchNumber = QC.BatchNumber
WHERE QC.Result = 'Fail'
GROUP BY PO.Status;

/* Query 2
Are there many QC tests in the data that were marked as performed before the scheduled date of the production order? 
Yes, there are approximately 170-200 tests from each stage of production. I will account for this error, as well as excluding 
scheduled Batch Numbers in general, as it doesn't make sense that a QC test was performed on a non-existent batch of products.  
*/
SELECT PO.Status, COUNT(DISTINCT PO.BatchNumber) AS NumberOfBatches
FROM ProductOrders PO
JOIN QCTests QC
ON PO.BatchNumber = QC.Batchnumber
WHERE TestDate < ScheduledDate
GROUP BY PO.Status;

/* Query 3
Repeating the orginal query with errors accounted for
14 completed batches failed, 10 batches in progress, and 13 batches on hold. 
*/
SELECT PO.Status, COUNT(DISTINCT PO.BatchNumber) AS NumberOfBatches
FROM ProductOrders PO
JOIN QCTests QC
ON PO.BatchNumber = QC.BatchNumber
WHERE QC.Result = 'Fail' 
AND PO.Status <> 'Scheduled'
AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY PO.Status;

/* Query 4
For completed production orders that have failed their QC tests, how much product is being wasted? 
Products P102 and C303 have the largest number of units, 3000 and 2500 respectively, failing the texture test. 
*/
SELECT QC.ProductID, QC.TestType,
SUM(PO.QuantityToProduce) AS TotalQuantity 
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE Result = 'Fail'
AND PO.Status = 'Completed'
AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.ProductID, QC.TestType
ORDER BY QC.ProductID;

/* Query 5
Which batches failed the most tests and which tests
It appears that each batch failed only one test each
*/
SELECT QC.BatchNumber, QC.ProductID, PO.QuantityToProduce, PO.Status, TestType, 
COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY QC.BatchNumber) AS NumberOfFailures
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE Result = 'Fail'
AND PO.Status <> 'Scheduled'
AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
ORDER BY PO.Status;

/* Query 6
What is the proportion of failures for each type of quality control tests?
At 6.2%, products fail the purity test more than the other three types of tests  
*/
WITH Failures AS (
SELECT QC.TestType, COUNT(*) AS NumberOfFails 
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE Result = 'Fail'
AND PO.Status <> 'Scheduled'
AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.TestType
)
SELECT QC.TestType,
ROUND(100*((F.NumberOfFails*1.0)/COUNT(*)),2) AS RateOfFailure
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN Failures F
ON QC.TestType = F.TestType
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE PO.Status <> 'Scheduled' AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.TestType
ORDER BY ROUND(100*((F.NumberOfFails*1.0)/COUNT(*)),2) DESC;

/* Query 7
What rate does each product fail at?
Products C303 and P202 have the highest failure rate at 8% and 7.7% respectively. 
*/
WITH Failures AS (
SELECT QC.ProductId, COUNT(*) AS NumberOfFails 
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE Result = 'Fail'
AND PO.Status <> 'Scheduled'
AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.ProductID
)
SELECT QC.ProductID,
ROUND(100*((F.NumberOfFails*1.0)/COUNT(*)),2) AS RateOfFailure
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN Failures F
ON QC.ProductID = F.ProductID
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE PO.Status <> 'Scheduled' AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.ProductID
ORDER BY ROUND(100*((F.NumberOfFails*1.0)/COUNT(*)),2) DESC;

/* Query 8
Which type of test are those two products failing the most?
Product C303 fails the taste test the most and product P202 fails the purity test the most 
*/
WITH Rankings AS (
SELECT QC.ProductID, QC.TestType, COUNT(*) AS NumberOfFailures,
RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY QC.ProductID ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC) AS RANK
FROM QCTests QC
JOIN ProductOrders PO
ON QC.BatchNumber = PO.BatchNumber
WHERE QC.ProductID IN ('P202','C303')
AND PO.Status <> 'Scheduled' AND TestDate >= ScheduledDate
GROUP BY QC.ProductID, QC.TestType
ORDER BY QC.ProductID, COUNT(*) DESC
)
SELECT ProductID, TestType, NumberOfFailures
FROM Rankings
WHERE RANK = 1;

/* Query 9
Do any raw material supplies need to be reordered?
This query is written with the assumption that anything with a date older than the most recent order date is historical data 
No, there are not any raw materials that currently need to be ordered.  
*/
WITH RecentOrders AS (
SELECT *, 
RANK() OVER (
PARTITION BY MaterialID, SupplierID 
ORDER BY LastOrderDate DESC
) AS RANKS
FROM INVENTORY
)
SELECT MaterialID, MaterialName, SupplierID, QuantityOnHand, Unit, UnitCost, 
CASE WHEN ReorderPoint > QuantityOnHand THEN 'YES'
ELSE 'NO'
END AS Reorder
FROM RecentOrders
WHERE RANKS = 1;

/* Query 10
A report of the average cost of each material from each supplier, ordered from least to most expensive 
Supplier 1 is the cheapest option for three out of the five raw materials. Supplier 2 is the least expensive for Cane Sugar
and Supplier 3 has the cheapest Whole Milk Powder. 
*/
SELECT MaterialName, SupplierID, ROUND(AVG(UnitCost),2) AS AverageCost
FROM INVENTORY
GROUP BY MaterialName, SupplierID
ORDER BY MaterialName, AVG(UnitCost);

/* Query 11
Which shipping carrier is the least expensive to use?
ChocoExpress is the cheapest carrier on average 
*/
SELECT Carrier, ROUND(AVG(ShippingCost),2) AS AverageCost
FROM SHIPMENTS 
GROUP BY Carrier
ORDER BY AVG(ShippingCost) ASC; 

/* Query 12
Does the carrier have an effect on whether a shipment will be delayed or not?
ChocoExpress has the highest percentage of delays; however only by a modest amount. 
I will investigate the shipment origins and destinations next 
*/
WITH DELAYS AS (
SELECT Carrier, COUNT(ShipmentID) AS TotalDelays
FROM SHIPMENTS
WHERE Status = 'Delayed' 
OR ActualDeliveryDate > ExpectedDeliveryDate
GROUP BY Carrier
)
SELECT S.Carrier, D.TotalDelays,
ROUND(100*((TotalDelays*1.0)/COUNT(ShipmentID)),2) AS DelayPercentage
FROM SHIPMENTS S
JOIN DELAYS D
ON S.Carrier = D.Carrier
GROUP BY S.Carrier;

-- Query 13
-- Shipments sent from Bangalore and Chicago have a delay rate of 43%
WITH DELAYS AS (
SELECT Origin, COUNT(ShipmentID) AS TotalDelays
FROM SHIPMENTS
WHERE Status = 'Delayed' 
OR ActualDeliveryDate > ExpectedDeliveryDate
GROUP BY Origin
)
SELECT S.Origin, D.TotalDelays,
ROUND(100*((TotalDelays*1.0)/COUNT(ShipmentID)),2) AS DelayPercentage
FROM SHIPMENTS S
JOIN DELAYS D
ON S.Origin = D.Origin
GROUP BY S.Origin;

-- Query 14
-- Shipments destined for Berlin and Tokyo have a delay rate of 45% and 44% respectively 
WITH DELAYS AS (
SELECT Destination, COUNT(ShipmentID) AS TotalDelays
FROM SHIPMENTS
WHERE Status = 'Delayed' 
OR ActualDeliveryDate > ExpectedDeliveryDate
GROUP BY Destination
)
SELECT S.Destination, D.TotalDelays,
ROUND(100*((TotalDelays*1.0)/COUNT(ShipmentID)),2) AS DelayPercentage
FROM SHIPMENTS S
JOIN DELAYS D
ON S.Destination = D.Destination
GROUP BY S.Destination;

/* Query 15
Berlin to Berlin oddly enough has the highest proportion of delays at 75%
Buenos Aires to Toronto, Chicago to Toronto, and London to Berlin are tied for the second most proportion of delays at 67%. 
*/
WITH DELAYS AS (
SELECT CONCAT(Origin,' to ', Destination) AS Path, COUNT(ShipmentID) AS TotalDelays
FROM SHIPMENTS
WHERE Status = 'Delayed' 
OR ActualDeliveryDate > ExpectedDeliveryDate
GROUP BY CONCAT(Origin,' to ', Destination)
)
SELECT CONCAT(S.Origin,' to ', S.Destination) AS Path, D.TotalDelays,
ROUND(100*((TotalDelays*1.0)/COUNT(ShipmentID)),2) AS DelayPercentage
FROM SHIPMENTS S
JOIN DELAYS D
ON CONCAT(S.Origin,' to ', S.Destination) = D.Path
GROUP BY CONCAT(S.Origin,' to ', S.Destination);

/* Query 16
Since Berlin seems to be an issue, let's see if we should stop using a specific carrier when shipping to Berlin
It seems that ChocoExpress has the highest rate of delays when shipping to Berlin
*/
WITH DELAYS AS (
SELECT Carrier, COUNT(ShipmentID) AS TotalDelays
FROM SHIPMENTS
WHERE 
(Status = 'Delayed' 
OR ActualDeliveryDate > ExpectedDeliveryDate)
AND Destination = 'Berlin'
GROUP BY Carrier
)
SELECT S.Carrier, D.TotalDelays,
ROUND(100*((TotalDelays*1.0)/COUNT(ShipmentID)),2) AS DelayPercentage
FROM SHIPMENTS S
JOIN DELAYS D
ON S.Carrier = D.Carrier
WHERE Destination = 'Berlin'
GROUP BY S.Carrier;

/* Query 17
-How long does it take shipments to be delivered along each route?
Toronto to Paris is the shortest route, with Paris to Buenos Aires as the longest. 
*/
SELECT CONCAT(Origin,' to ', Destination) AS Route,
CASE 
WHEN Status = 'Delivered' THEN ROUND(AVG(JULIANDAY(ActualDeliveryDate)-JULIANDAY(ShipDate)),1)
ELSE ROUND(AVG(JULIANDAY(ExpectedDeliveryDate)-JULIANDAY(ShipDate)),1)
END AS AverageDays
FROM SHIPMENTS
GROUP BY CONCAT(Origin,' to ', Destination);
